## sn41.6
sn41.6:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Kāmabhū was staying near Macchikāsaṇḍa in the Wild Mango Grove. | T: On one occasion Ven. Kāmabhū was staying near Macchikāsaṇḍa in the Wild Mango Grove.
sn41.6:1.2 | S: Then Citta the householder went up to Venerable Kāmabhū, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then Citta the householder went to him and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Kāmabhū,
sn41.6:1.3 | S: “Sir, how many processes are there?” | T: “Venerable sir, how many (types of) fabrications are there?”
sn41.6:1.4 | S: “Householder, there are three processes. | T: “There are three fabrications, householder:
sn41.6:1.5 | S: The physical process, the verbal process, and the mental process.” | T: bodily-fabrications, verbal fabrications, & mental fabrications.”
sn41.6:1.6 | S: Saying “Good, sir,” Citta approved and agreed with what Kāmabhū said. Then he asked another question: | T: “Very good, venerable sir.” And, delighting in and approving of Ven. Kāmabhū’s answer, Citta asked him a further question:
sn41.6:1.7 | S: “But sir, what is the physical process? What’s the verbal process? What’s the mental process?” | T: “But what are bodily-fabrications? What are verbal fabrications? What are mental fabrications?”
sn41.6:1.8 | S: “Breathing is a physical process. Placing the mind and keeping it connected are a verbal process. Perception and feeling are a mental process.” | T: “In-&-out breaths are bodily fabrications. Directed thought & evaluation are verbal fabrications. Perceptions & feelings are mental fabrications.”
sn41.6:2.1 | S: Saying “Good, sir,” he asked another question: | T: “Very good, venerable sir.” And, delighting in and approving of Ven. Kāmabhū’s answer, Citta asked him a further question:
sn41.6:2.2 | S: “But sir, why is breathing a physical process? Why are placing the mind and keeping it connected a verbal process? Why are perception and feeling a mental process?” | T: “But why are in-&-out breaths bodily fabrications? Why are directed thought & evaluation verbal fabrications? Why are perceptions & feelings mental fabrications?”
sn41.6:2.3 | S: “Breathing is physical. It’s tied up with the body, that’s why breathing is a physical process. | T: “In-&-out breaths are bodily; these are things tied up with the body. That’s why in-&-out breaths are bodily fabrications.
sn41.6:2.4 | S: First you place the mind and keep it connected, then you break into speech. That’s why placing the mind and keeping it connected are a verbal process. | T: Having first directed one’s thoughts and made an evaluation, one then breaks out into speech. That’s why directed thought & evaluation are verbal fabrications.
sn41.6:2.5 | S: Perception and feeling are mental. They’re tied up with the mind, that’s why perception and feeling are a mental process.” | T: Perceptions & feelings are mental; these are things tied up with the mind. That’s why perceptions & feelings are mental fabrications.”
sn41.6:3.1 | S: Saying “Good, sir,” he asked another question: | T: “Very good, venerable sir.” And, delighting in and approving of Ven. Kāmabhū’s answer, Citta asked him a further question:
sn41.6:3.2 | S: “But sir, how does someone attain the cessation of perception and feeling?” | T: “Now, how does the attainment of the cessation of perception & feeling come about?”
sn41.6:3.3 | S: “A mendicant who is entering such an attainment does not think: | T: “The thought does not occur to a monk as he is attaining the cessation of perception & feeling that ‘I am about to attain the cessation of perception & feeling’ or that ‘I am attaining the cessation of perception & feeling’ or that ‘I have attained the cessation of perception & feeling.’
sn41.6:3.4 | S: ‘I will enter the cessation of perception and feeling’ or ‘I am entering the cessation of perception and feeling’ or ‘I have entered the cessation of perception and feeling.’ | T: (none)
sn41.6:3.5 | S: Rather, their mind has been previously developed so as to lead to such a state.” | T: Instead, the way his mind has previously been developed leads him to that state.”
sn41.6:4.1 | S: Saying “Good, sir,” he asked another question: | T: “Very good, venerable sir.” And, delighting in and approving of Ven. Kāmabhū’s answer, Citta asked him a further question:
sn41.6:4.2 | S: “But sir, which cease first for a mendicant who is entering the cessation of perception and feeling: the physical, verbal, or mental process?” | T: “When a monk is attaining the cessation of perception & feeling, which things cease first: bodily fabrications, verbal fabrications, or mental fabrications?”
sn41.6:4.3 | S: “The verbal process ceases first, then physical, then mental.” | T: “When a monk is attaining the cessation of perception & feeling, verbal fabrications cease first, then bodily fabrications, then mental fabrications.”
sn41.6:5.1 | S: Saying “Good, sir,” he asked another question: | T: “Very good, venerable sir.” And, delighting in and approving of Ven. Kāmabhū’s answer, Citta asked him a further question:
sn41.6:5.2 | S: “What’s the difference between someone who has passed away and a mendicant who has attained the cessation of perception and feeling?” | T: “What is the difference between a monk who has died & passed away and a monk who has attained the cessation of perception & feeling?”
sn41.6:5.3 | S: “When someone dies, their physical, verbal, and mental processes have ceased and stilled; their vitality is spent; their warmth is dissipated; and their faculties have disintegrated. | T: “In the case of a monk who has died & passed away, his bodily fabrication has ceased & subsided, verbal fabrication has ceased & subsided, mental fabrication has ceased & subsided, his life force is totally ended, his heat is dissipated, and his faculties are shut down.
sn41.6:5.4 | S: When a mendicant has attained the cessation of perception and feeling, their physical, verbal, and mental processes have ceased and stilled. But their vitality is not spent; their warmth is not dissipated; and their faculties are very clear. | T: But in the case of a monk who has attained the cessation of perception & feeling, his bodily fabrication has ceased & subsided, verbal fabrication has ceased & subsided, mental fabrication has ceased & subsided, his life force is not ended, his heat is not dissipated, and his faculties are bright & clear.
sn41.6:5.5 | S: That’s the difference between someone who has passed away and a mendicant who has attained the cessation of perception and feeling.” | T: This is the difference between a monk who has died & passed away and a monk who has attained the cessation of perception & feeling.”
sn41.6:6.1 | S: Saying “Good, sir,” he asked another question: | T: “Very good, venerable sir.” And, delighting in and approving of Ven. Kāmabhū’s answer, Citta asked him a further question:
sn41.6:6.2 | S: “But sir, how does someone emerge from the cessation of perception and feeling?” | T: “Now, how does emergence from the cessation of perception & feeling come about?”
sn41.6:6.3 | S: “A mendicant who is emerging from such an attainment does not think: | T: “The thought does not occur to a monk as he is emerging from the cessation of perception & feeling that
sn41.6:6.4 | S: ‘I will emerge from the cessation of perception and feeling’ or ‘I am emerging from the cessation of perception and feeling’ or ‘I have emerged from the cessation of perception and feeling.’ | T: ‘I am about to emerge from the cessation of perception & feeling’ or that ‘I am emerging from the cessation of perception & feeling’ or that ‘I have emerged from the cessation of perception & feeling.’
sn41.6:6.5 | S: Rather, their mind has been previously developed so as to lead to such a state.” | T: Instead, the way his mind has previously been developed leads him to that state.”
sn41.6:7.1 | S: Saying “Good, sir,” he asked another question: | T: “Very good, venerable sir.” And, delighting in and approving of Ven. Kāmabhū’s answer, Citta asked him a further question:
sn41.6:7.2 | S: “But sir, which arise first for a mendicant who is emerging from the cessation of perception and feeling: the physical, verbal, or mental process?” | T: “When a monk is emerging from the cessation of perception & feeling, which things arise first: bodily fabrications, verbal fabrications, or mental fabrications?”
sn41.6:7.3 | S: “The mental process arises first, then physical, then verbal.” | T: “When a monk is emerging from the cessation of perception & feeling, mental fabrications arise first, then bodily fabrications, then verbal fabrications.”
sn41.6:8.1 | S: Saying “Good, sir,” he asked another question: | T: “Very good, venerable sir.” And, delighting in and approving of Ven. Kāmabhū’s answer, Citta asked him a further question:
sn41.6:8.2 | S: “But sir, when a mendicant has emerged from the attainment of the cessation of perception and feeling, how many kinds of contact do they experience?” | T: “When a monk has emerged from the cessation of perception & feeling, how many contacts make contact?”
sn41.6:8.3 | S: “They experience three kinds of contact: | T: “When a monk has emerged from the cessation of perception & feeling, three contacts make contact:
sn41.6:8.4 | S: emptiness, signless, and undirected contacts.” | T: contact with emptiness, contact with the themeless, & contact with the undirected.”
sn41.6:9.1 | S: Saying “Good, sir,” he asked another question: | T: “Very good, venerable sir.” And, delighting in and approving of Ven. Kāmabhū’s answer, Citta asked him a further question:
sn41.6:9.2 | S: “But sir, when a mendicant has emerged from the attainment of the cessation of perception and feeling, what does their mind slant, slope, and incline to?” | T: “When a monk has emerged from the cessation of perception & feeling, to what does his mind lean, to what does it tend, to what does it incline?”
sn41.6:9.3 | S: “Their mind slants, slopes, and inclines to seclusion.” | T: “When a monk has emerged from the cessation of perception & feeling, his mind leans to seclusion, tends to seclusion, inclines to seclusion.”
sn41.6:10.1 | S: Saying “Good, sir,” Citta approved and agreed with what Kāmabhū said. Then he asked another question: | T: “Very good, venerable sir.” And, delighting in and approving of Ven. Kāmabhū’s answer, Citta asked him a further question:
sn41.6:10.2 | S: “But sir, how many things are helpful for attaining the cessation of perception and feeling?” | T: “How many mental qualities are of great help in the attainment of the cessation of perception & feeling?”
sn41.6:10.3 | S: “Well, householder, you’ve finally asked what you should have asked first! | T: “Actually, householder, you have asked last what should have been asked first.
sn41.6:10.4 | S: Nevertheless, I will answer you. | T: Nevertheless, I will answer you.
sn41.6:10.5 | S: Two things are helpful for attaining the cessation of perception and feeling: | T: Two qualities are of great help in the attainment of the cessation of perception & feeling:
sn41.6:10.6 | S: serenity and discernment.” | T: tranquility & insight.”
sn41.6:10.7 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

