an10.99:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Upāli went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: Then Ven. Upāli went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One,
an10.99:1.2 | S: “Sir, I wish to frequent remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest.” | T: “Lord, I want to spend time in isolated wilderness & forest lodgings.”
an10.99:2.1 | S: “Upāli, remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest are challenging. | T: “Upāli, it’s not easy to endure isolated wilderness & forest lodgings.
an10.99:2.2 | S: It’s hard to maintain seclusion and hard to find joy in it. | T: It’s not easy to maintain seclusion, not easy to enjoy being alone.
an10.99:2.3 | S: Staying alone, the forests seem to rob the mind of a mendicant who isn’t immersed in samādhi. | T: The forests, as it were, plunder the mind of a monk who has not gained concentration.
an10.99:2.4 | S: If someone should say this, | T: Whoever would say,
an10.99:2.5 | S: ‘Though I don’t have immersion, I’m going to frequent remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest.’ You can expect that | T: ‘I, without having gained concentration, will spend time in isolated wilderness & forest lodgings,’ of him it can be expected that
an10.99:2.6 | S: they’ll sink down or float away. | T: he will sink to the bottom or float away.
an10.99:3.1 | S: Suppose there was a large lake, | T: “Imagine, Upāli, a great freshwater lake.
an10.99:3.2 | S: and along comes a bull elephant that’s three or three and a half meters tall. | T: Then there would come a great bull elephant, seven or seven and a half cubits tall.
an10.99:3.3 | S: He’d think, | T: The thought would occur to him,
an10.99:3.4 | S: ‘Why don’t I plunge into this lake and play around while washing my ears and back? | T: ‘What if I were to plunge into this freshwater lake, to playfully squirt water into my ears and along my back, and then—having playfully squirted water into my ears and along my back,
an10.99:3.5 | S: When I’ve bathed and drunk, I’ll emerge from the water and go wherever I want.’ | T: having bathed & drunk & come back out—to go off as I please?’
an10.99:3.6 | S: And that’s just what he does. | T: So, having plunged into the freshwater lake, he would
an10.99:3.7 | P: kaṇṇasandhovikampi khiḍḍaṁ kīḷitvā piṭṭhisandhovikampi khiḍḍaṁ kīḷitvā nhatvā ca pivitvā ca paccuttaritvā yena kāmaṁ pakkameyya. | T: playfully squirt water into his ears and along his back, and then—having playfully squirted water into his ears and along his back, having bathed & drunk & come back out—he would go off as he pleased.
an10.99:3.8 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an10.99:3.9 | S: Because his large incarnation finds a footing in the depths. | T: Because his large body finds a footing in the depth.
an10.99:4.1 | S: Then along comes a rabbit or a cat. | T: “Then a rabbit or a cat would come along.
an10.99:4.2 | S: They’d think, | T: The thought would occur to it,
an10.99:4.3 | S: ‘What difference is there between me and a bull elephant? | T: ‘What’s the difference between me and a bull elephant?
an10.99:4.4 | S: Why don’t I plunge into this lake and play around while washing my ears and back? | T: What if I were to plunge into this freshwater lake, to playfully squirt water into my ears and along my back, and then—having playfully squirted water into my ears and along my back,
an10.99:4.5 | S: When I’ve bathed and drunk, I’ll emerge from the water and go wherever I want.’ | T: having bathed & drunk & come back out—to go off as I please?’
an10.99:4.6 | S: They jump into the lake rashly, without thinking. | T: So, without reflecting, he jumps rashly into the freshwater lake,
an10.99:4.7 | S: You can expect that | T: and of him it can be expected that he will either sink to the bottom or float away.
