an10.99:4.13 | S: they’ll sink down or float away. | T: he will sink to the bottom or float away.
an10.99:5.1 | S: Suppose there was a little baby boy playing in his own urine and feces. | T: “Imagine, Upāli, a stupid baby boy, lying on his back, playing with his urine & excrement.
an10.99:5.2 | S: What do you think, Upāli? | T: What do you think?
an10.99:5.3 | S: Isn’t that a totally foolish game?” | T: Isn’t that totally & completely a childish form of play?”
an10.99:5.4 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
an10.99:6.1 | S: “After some time that boy grows up and his faculties mature. | T: “Then, as the child grows and his faculties mature,
an10.99:6.2 | S: He accordingly plays childish games such as toy plows, tip-cat, somersaults, pinwheels, toy measures, toy carts, and toy bows. | T: he plays at children’s games: toy plows, stick games, somersaults, toy windmills, toy measures, toy carts, and a toy bow & arrow.
an10.99:6.3 | S: What do you think, Upāli? | T: What do you think?
an10.99:6.4 | S: Aren’t such games better than what he did before?” | T: Aren’t these forms of play more excellent than the earlier form of play, and more refined?”
an10.99:6.5 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
an10.99:7.1 | S: “After some time that boy grows up and his faculties mature further. | T: “At a later time, as the boy grows and his faculties mature (still further),
an10.99:7.2 | S: He accordingly amuses himself, supplied and provided with the five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: he enjoys himself provided & endowed with the five strings of sensuality:
an10.99:7.3 | S: Sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: forms cognizable via the eye—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, accompanied with sensual desire;
an10.99:7.4 | S: Sounds known by the ear … | T: sounds cognizable via the ear…
an10.99:7.5 | S: Smells known by the nose … | T: aromas cognizable via the nose…
an10.99:7.6 | S: Tastes known by the tongue … | T: flavors cognizable via the tongue…
an10.99:7.7 | S: Touches known by the body, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: tactile sensations cognizable via the body—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, accompanied with sensual desire.
an10.99:7.8 | S: What do you think, Upāli? | T: What do you think?
an10.99:7.9 | S: Aren’t such games better than what he did before?” | T: Aren’t these forms of play more excellent than the earlier forms of play, and more refined?”
an10.99:7.10 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
an10.99:8.1 | S: “Now in this regard, Upāli, a Realized One arises in the world, perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed. | T: “Now, Upāli, there is the case where a Tathāgata appears in the world, worthy & rightly self-awakened.
an10.99:8.2 | S: He has realized with his own insight this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, gods and humans—and he makes it known to others. | T: (none)
an10.99:8.3 | S: He teaches Dhamma that’s good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaningful and well-phrased. And he reveals a spiritual practice that’s entirely full and pure. | T: He teaches the Dhamma admirable in its beginning, admirable in its middle, admirable in its end. He proclaims the holy life both in its particulars and in its essence, entirely perfect, surpassingly pure.
an10.99:9.1 | S: A householder hears that teaching, or a householder’s child, or someone reborn in a good family. | T: “A householder or householder’s son or one born in one clan or another hears that Dhamma.
an10.99:9.2 | S: They gain faith in the Realized One | T: Having heard the Dhamma, he gains conviction in the Tathāgata. Being endowed with a gain in conviction,
an10.99:9.3 | S: and reflect: | T: he reflects:
an10.99:9.4 | S: ‘Life at home is cramped and dirty, life gone forth is wide open. | T: ‘Household life is confining, a dusty path. Life gone forth is the open air.
an10.99:9.5 | S: It’s not easy for someone living at home to lead the spiritual life utterly full and pure, like a polished shell. | T: It isn’t easy, living at home, to practice the holy life totally perfect, totally pure, a polished shell.
an10.99:9.6 | S: Why don’t I shave off my hair and beard, dress in ocher robes, and go forth from the lay life to homelessness?’ | T: What if I, having shaved off my hair & beard and putting on the ochre robe, were to go forth from the household life into homelessness?’
an10.99:10.1 | S: After some time they give up a large or small fortune, and a large or small family circle. They shave off hair and beard, dress in ocher robes, and go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: “So after some time he abandons his mass of wealth, large or small; leaves his circle of relatives, large or small; shaves off his hair & beard, puts on the ochre robes, and goes forth from the household life into homelessness.
an10.99:11.1 | S: Once they’ve gone forth, they take up the training and livelihood of the mendicants. They give up killing living creatures, renouncing the rod and the sword. They’re scrupulous and kind, living full of sympathy for all living beings. | T: Virtue “When he has thus gone forth, endowed with the monks’ training & livelihood, then—abandoning the taking of life—he abstains from the taking of life. He dwells with his rod laid down, his knife laid down, scrupulous, merciful, compassionate for the welfare of all living beings.
an10.99:12.1 | S: They give up stealing. They take only what’s given, and expect only what’s given. They keep themselves clean by not thieving. | T: “Abandoning the taking of what is not given, he abstains from taking what is not given. He takes only what is given, accepts only what is given, lives not by stealth but by means of a self that has become pure. This, too, is part of his virtue.
an10.99:13.1 | S: They give up unchastity. They are chaste, set apart, refraining from the vulgar act of sex. | T: “Abandoning uncelibacy, he lives a celibate life, aloof, refraining from the sexual act that is the villager’s way.
an10.99:14.1 | S: They give up lying. They speak the truth and stick to the truth. They’re honest and dependable, and don’t trick the world with their words. | T: “Abandoning the telling of lies, he abstains from telling lies. He speaks the truth, holds to the truth, is firm, reliable, no deceiver of the world.
an10.99:15.1 | S: They give up backbiting. They don’t repeat in one place what they heard in another so as to divide people against each other. Instead, they reconcile those who are divided and support those who are united, delighting in harmony, loving harmony, speaking words that promote harmony. | T: “Abandoning divisive speech, he abstains from divisive speech. What he has heard here he does not tell there to break those people apart from these people here. What he has heard there he does not tell here to break these people apart from those people there. Thus reconciling those who have broken apart or cementing those who are united, he loves concord, delights in concord, enjoys concord, speaks things that create concord.
an10.99:16.1 | S: They give up harsh speech. They speak in a way that’s mellow, pleasing to the ear, endearing, going to the heart, polite, and likable and agreeable to the people. | T: “Abandoning abusive speech, he abstains from abusive speech. He speaks words that are soothing to the ear, that are affectionate, that go to the heart, that are polite, appealing & pleasing to people at large.
an10.99:17.1 | S: They give up talking nonsense. Their words are timely, true, and meaningful, in line with the teaching and training. They say things at the right time which are valuable, reasonable, succinct, and beneficial. | T: “Abandoning idle chatter, he abstains from idle chatter. He speaks in season, speaks what is factual, what is in accordance with the goal, the Dhamma, and the Vinaya. He speaks words worth treasuring, seasonable, reasonable, circumscribed, connected with the goal.
an10.99:18.1 | S: They refrain from injuring plants and seeds. | T: “He abstains from damaging seed & plant life.
an10.99:18.2 | S: They eat in one part of the day, abstaining from eating at night and food at the wrong time. | T: “He eats only once a day, refraining from the evening meal and from food at the wrong time of day.
an10.99:18.3 | S: They refrain from seeing shows of dancing, singing, and music. | T: “He abstains from dancing, singing, instrumental music, and from watching shows.
an10.99:18.4 | S: They refrain from attiring and adorning themselves with garlands, fragrance, and makeup. | T: “He abstains from wearing garlands and from beautifying himself with scents & cosmetics.
an10.99:18.5 | S: They refrain from high and luxurious beds. | T: “He abstains from high & luxurious beds & seats.
an10.99:18.6 | S: They refrain from receiving gold and currency, | T: “He abstains from accepting gold & money.
an10.99:18.7 | S: raw grains, | T: “He abstains from accepting uncooked grain…
an10.99:18.8 | S: raw meat, | T: raw meat…
an10.99:18.9 | S: women and girls, | T: women & girls…
an10.99:18.10 | S: male and female bondservants, | T: male & female slaves…
an10.99:18.11 | S: goats and sheep, | T: goats & sheep…
an10.99:18.12 | S: chickens and pigs, | T: fowl & pigs…
an10.99:18.13 | S: elephants, cows, horses, and mares, | T: elephants, cattle, steeds, & mares…
an10.99:18.14 | S: and fields and land. | T: fields & property.
an10.99:18.15 | S: They refrain from running errands and messages; | T: “He abstains from running messages…
an10.99:18.16 | S: buying and selling; | T: from buying & selling…
an10.99:18.17 | S: falsifying weights, metals, or measures; | T: from dealing with false scales, false metals, & false measures…
an10.99:18.18 | S: bribery, fraud, cheating, and duplicity; | T: from bribery, deception, & fraud.
an10.99:18.19 | S: mutilation, murder, abduction, banditry, plunder, and violence. | T: “He abstains from mutilating, executing, imprisoning, highway robbery, plunder, & violence.
an10.99:19.1 | S: They’re content with robes to look after the body and almsfood to look after the belly. | T: “He is content with a set of robes to provide for his body and alms food to provide for his hunger.
an10.99:19.2 | S: Wherever they go, they set out taking only these things. | T: (none)
an10.99:19.3 | S: They’re like a bird: wherever it flies, wings are its only burden. | T: Just as a bird, wherever it goes, flies with its wings as its only burden;
an10.99:19.4 | S: In the same way, a mendicant is content with robes to look after the body and almsfood to look after the belly. Wherever they go, they set out taking only these things. | T: so too is he content with a set of robes to provide for his body and alms food to provide for his hunger. Wherever he goes, he takes only his barest necessities along.
an10.99:19.5 | S: When they have this spectrum of noble ethics, they experience a blameless happiness inside themselves. | T: “Endowed with this noble aggregate of virtue, he is inwardly sensitive to the pleasure of being blameless.
an10.99:20.1 | S: When they see a sight with their eyes, they don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: Sense Restraint “On seeing a form with the eye, he doesn’t grasp at any theme or details by which—
an10.99:20.2 | S: If the faculty of sight were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, they practice restraint, protecting the faculty of sight, and achieving its restraint. | T: if he were to dwell without restraint over the faculty of the eye—evil, unskillful qualities such as greed or distress might assail him. He practices for its restraint. He protects the faculty of the eye. He attains restraint with regard to the faculty of the eye.
an10.99:20.3 | S: When they hear a sound with their ears … | T: On hearing a sound with the ear.…
an10.99:20.4 | S: When they smell an odor with their nose … | T: On smelling an aroma with the nose.…
an10.99:20.5 | S: When they taste a flavor with their tongue … | T: On tasting a flavor with the tongue.…
an10.99:20.6 | S: When they feel a touch with their body … | T: On touching a tactile sensation with the body.…
an10.99:20.7 | S: When they know an idea with their mind, they don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: On cognizing an idea with the intellect, he doesn’t grasp at any theme or details by which—
